## Quantity surface contract · `sec:matrix:quantity-surface`

**Definition (Quantity value over the existing heap)** · `def:matrix:quantity-value`

A continuous quantity is a selected region handle together with its checked bindings descriptor reference and captured declaration and value versions. The quantity adapter retains the existing `ValueBinding` and its complete companion inventory; it owns no second center array, dimension algebra or uncertainty record. A scalar has extent 1×1 and an array retains its checked rectangle and logical selection. Every access revalidates the original symbol and numerical tickets under (`sec:matrix:concepts`), (`sec:matrix:reading-adapters`); the root statement “Descriptor unit authority” assigns physical meaning to bindings.

**Rule (Quantity admission before numerical access)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-admission`

A quantity operation checks the original descriptors, dimension, semantic kind, affine roles, ordered extents and requested result contract through bindings before borrowing coordinates. Original declaration and expression evidence remains owned by its checked snapshot; a zero, cancellation or equal-dimension result cannot erase a refused operand. Only a closed supported operation can select an existing matrix kernel. Equal descriptors cannot substitute a foreign declaration, source or live version under (`def:matrix:quantity-value`), (`sec:matrix:guarantees`); the root statements “Plan validation” and “Checked lowering” retain their separate original-expression and operation-correspondence obligations.

**Rule (Point and difference operations)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-affine`

Compatible point minus point produces a difference; point plus or minus difference produces a point; difference plus point produces a point; difference arithmetic preserves its admitted kind. Point plus point, difference minus point and multiplicative point operations refuse at descriptor admission. A common numerical coordinate measure must be established explicitly before a sum kernel runs, and a zero-offset point remains a point. The adapter uses the bindings role table and the existing sum and conversion kernels under (`rule:matrix:quantity-admission`), (`sec:matrix:guarantees`), implementing the root statement “Measure conversions and point arithmetic”.

**Rule (Declared origins survive removal and restoration)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-origin`

Origin removal requires a compatible declared point origin and produces a difference from that exact origin; restoration requires the same retained origin and returns a point. A normalization additionally retains its positive typed reference and exact inverse. The origin shifts only centers and contributes its numerical evaluation error; it never shifts a radius or covariance. Converting Celsius points to kelvin coordinates does not remove their role. These operations retain the maps and source versions of (`rule:matrix:quantity-affine`), (`sec:matrix:guarantees`) and the root statement “Reference scales are explicit exact definitions”.

**Rule (Exact discrete storage and continuous expectations)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-counts`

Discrete quantities retain an exact integer in separately checked storage, the declared counted kind and sign/range domain, and their bindings evidence; they cannot enter a floating arena. Checked count arithmetic tests both operand meanings, exact integrality, the actual result domain and representation overflow before publication. Fractional quotients, overflowing results and an implicit count-to-ratio cast refuse. An explicit same-kind count ratio produces a continuous ratio with conversion error when numerically represented. An expectation or standard uncertainty is a separately declared continuous quantity and does not weaken the original count contract. This is the discrete implementation obligation beside (`def:matrix:quantity-value`), (`rule:matrix:quantity-admission`), following the root statements “Counts” and “Counts and structural metadata”.

**Rule (Measure conversions retain every companion)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-measure-conversion`

A measure conversion retains the bindings-checked exact affine map and invokes `ConversionContract` through the existing exclusive execution and atomic publication transaction. Absolute total and numerical-error channels scale by the absolute scale ratio, with new coefficient and evaluation error included; the total already contains the numerical channel. Covariance uses both ordered target scales, retains both target versions and carries its own parameter evaluation error; affine origins enter neither axis. Relative expressions retain their explicit reference. Unsupported companion modes refuse before publication instead of discarding a channel. These obligations refine (`rule:matrix:quantity-origin`), (`sec:matrix:reading-adapters`), (`sec:matrix:guarantees`) under the root statement “Uncertainty unit algebra”.

**Rule (Precision conversions retain nominal error)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-precision-conversion`

A precision conversion creates a fresh typed region through the existing matrix conversion kernels, preserves dimension, measure, kind, affine role and source coverage, and retargets every companion to the new version. Widening preserves prior numerical error as well as the total bound; narrowing additionally encloses representation displacement. The numerical-only channel continues to refer to the original nominal target across repeated conversions and is never added twice to the total. The quantity route must refuse a conversion that cannot satisfy this contract under (`rule:matrix:quantity-measure-conversion`), (`def:matrix:quantity-value`); the root statement “Precision conversions” does not permit recovery of lost information.

**Rule (Quantity refusals and resource accounting)** · `rule:matrix:quantity-refusals`

