Narrowing original phase widths and the shared clock offset by one half gives known-phase actual horizon `200*pi/3`, unknown-phase actual horizon `-16/3+200*pi/3`, and reported horizon `4/3+200*pi/3`. The complete refinement certificate checks original-question inclusion, exact product supports and a nonnegative finite-horizon difference at the same future reference (`test:integration:lc-relation-clock-refinement-horizons`). Narrow original timestamps with both error layers give actual horizons `39999*pi/400` and `3/2+2499*pi/100`, and reported horizons `-1+39999*pi/400` and `1+2499*pi/100` (`test:integration:lc-relation-both-timing-refinement-horizons`). Both formats retain the exact refined source and coordinate and enclose the independently specified affine-pi endpoints against one prepared Machin oracle.

## Prepared exponential measurements · `sec:matrix:prepared-exponential-measurements`

**Procedure (Standalone prepared phase measurement)** · `alg:matrix:prepared-exponential-measurement`

The `prepared_exponential_measurements` example follows the standalone LC acceptance harness: `cargo run --locked --release -p nrz-matrix --example prepared_exponential_measurements -- 256 disabled` measures a finite population; replace `disabled` with `enabled` to collect bounded tracing receipts. The cell count is a power of two from 4 through 4096, matching the fixture’s default matrix-entry allowance. An odd permutation supplies distinct, unsorted represented inputs, including zero and cancellation-sensitive points. Inputs precede all native clocks; every numerical output is consumed. Environment identity belongs in `NRZ_MEASUREMENT_ENV` and the accompanying process record: tested public commit, exact command, compiler, profile, target features, CPU affinity, cgroup, start/end load and pressure, process CPU and RSS. Repeated populations and uncertainty accompany any scaling comparison; no wall observation becomes a deterministic Work charge.

**Rule (Prepared measurement completion boundaries)** · `rule:matrix:prepared-measurement-boundaries`

Cold and warm CAS preparation and changed-target preparation end at the checked authority. Native admission ends at the frozen plan; coefficient import has its own nested span. The harness brackets exp admission with an existing Work phase, including the auxiliary-policy charge before the basis emits its first snapshot. Fresh-store replay ends at independently checked publication. Binary64 input import constructs represented point operands with identity nearest import; it does not measure arbitrary rational conversion. Native reuse ends after consuming complete readings and storing their ordered bit snapshots in storage reserved before the clock; batch evaluation ends at the materialized ordered result vector. The reading clock also stores complete batch snapshots in preallocated storage. Snapshots retain endpoint, centre, total-radius and numerical-error bits, or the typed refusal; they are harness storage, distinct from numerical-owner byte figures. Reading and release have separate clocks. The exp validation span identifies the repeated basis evaluation, without removing it or counting its inclusive time twice. Broad-box and three-refusal controls are separate from the varying-point comparison; successful broad-box readings pass through the same consumption barrier, and all four outcomes have complete snapshots. Platform binary64 `exp` and `exp_m1` use the same points and are labelled uncertified baselines, never reference computations. Native-zero evidence also requires lifetime-aware terminal coverage: each observed ledger must have a witnessed closure, or a last snapshot at or after the epoch advanced by the last native exit. A closed incarnation cannot consume further Work; its final delta is still subject to epoch invalidation or local attribution. Surviving ledgers use existing zero-Work phases to supply terminal snapshots when their ordinary operation does not. Measurements may not nest inside a native measurement boundary; such nesting invalidates observation even without Work. Kernel and validation spans remain supported inside those boundaries. The scalar completion boundary excludes lowered regions, masks and managed physical allocation (`dec:matrix:prepared-expm1-standalone-scope`).

**Definition (Prepared route observation coverage)** · `def:matrix:prepared-measurement-coverage`

The private bounded subscriber projects entry/exit Work onto the nearest active interval of the same ledger, subtracts immediate children once, retains refused prefixes, and reports unclassified remainder and existing-debit transfer links. The private implementation follows the CAS test subscriber’s ledger projection; events arrive through the declared bindings route. The observer stays private to the executable. Fixed kernel, validation, coefficient-import and measurement span identities supply independent inclusive clocks keyed by their enclosing completion boundary. Construction and engine counters are events. Live spans, depth, ledger identities, receipt keys, clock keys, counter keys and transfer records have finite capacities, and retained strings have a 512-byte ceiling. Loss invalidates the receipt rather than changing arithmetic. Enabled native boundaries must have zero caller-ledger delta and zero observed increment on every ledger incarnation, including private creation/destruction snapshots; construction events are checked separately. Boundary/ledger increment keys are capacity bounded. Each measurement entry and exit advances a checked epoch; a positive ledger delta whose snapshots straddle an epoch change invalidates the receipt because its boundary cannot be determined. Unchanged snapshots permit idle ledgers to cross boundaries without loss. The observer retains the first native-entry epoch until observation ends. A ledger first observed with nonzero Work after that entry, whether the native boundary remains active or has exited, invalidates the receipt: no earlier snapshot establishes its prefix. A first zero value is admissible, and a snapshot before any native entry can establish a nonzero prefix. This constant-size history complements the bounded active-stack check that rejects every measurement nested inside a native boundary. The latest native-exit epoch is retained in constant-size observer state, and finalisation checks terminal coverage even when creation was not seen. An observed closure establishes lifetime coverage whenever it occurs; only unclosed incarnations require a snapshot after the last exit. This conservative rule retains raw ledger reconciliation but never assigns ambiguous consumption to the boundary of its later snapshot; it requires no production event or API. Enabled and disabled runs must have equal ordered complete result snapshots and exact Work, not merely equal centre checksums. The native-zero guarantee covers ledger incarnations the subscriber observes, together with the native loops’ caller-ledger delta checks. A ledger emitting no snapshot during observation is outside subscriber visibility without additional production instrumentation. Initial anchoring, epoch invalidation, nesting refusal, terminal coverage and native-boundary Work checks bound the observed evidence; complete-result and exact-Work equivalence provide separate regression controls. Logical retained authority bytes and materialized inventories are distinct from physical memory; unavailable byte readings remain absent. Process RSS includes fixture setup and the observer and cannot be assigned to one owner.

**Criterion (Measurement harness controls)** · `crit:matrix:prepared-measurement-controls`

Observation preserves ordered complete result snapshots, typed refusals and exact Work, exposes exp validation and coefficient import, reconciles every ledger and reports observer capacity loss (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-equivalence`). An independently specified tariff separates parent and child Work from a prior prefix and trailing remainder (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-attribution`). The maximum accepted population completes through printed rows under the unchanged default allowances, the next size is refused before the allocation-producing builder is called, and tested populations have distinct unsorted points (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-inputs`). An independent two-unit caller charge and one-unit dropped-private-ledger charge are both attributed to the native boundary, excluding a seven-unit prior prefix (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-native-work`). A private ledger created before a native boundary, charged inside and closed afterwards invalidates the receipt, while idle residence remains complete (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-crossing-work`). Every admitted power-of-two population completes with equal complete results in both observation modes (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-populations`). A pre-observation ledger first seen with positive Work inside or after native execution invalidates the receipt; its idle twins remain complete (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-anchoring`). A non-native measurement nested in a native boundary invalidates receipts with and without a charge (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-nesting`). A pre-observation ledger surviving the report without a terminal snapshot invalidates coverage; an idle terminal anchor succeeds, while a charged terminal anchor exposes the existing epoch-crossing refusal (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-terminal`). An anchor between two native exits cannot cover the later exit, even with repeated boundary names (`test:integration:prepared-exponential-measurement-last-exit`). Numerical qualification remains with the independent endpoint and nominal controls and the structurally read golden values (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-native-oracles`), (`test:integration:prepared-exp-independent-oracles`), (`test:integration:prepared-exp-golden-values`). Recorded observations require an identified public tested commit and are separate from this executable's live output.

## Prepared binary64 expm1 bridge · `sec:matrix:prepared-expm1`

**Decision (Prepare and freeze authority)** · `dec:matrix:prepared-expm1-owner`

`PreparedExpm1::new` calls `Cas::prepare_expm1` through the bindings re-export, takes the plan actually returned, checks a requested represented subdomain against its original exact claim, and lowers its checked coefficients and export parameters. The returned owner borrows that CAS immutably for its entire lifetime: callers cannot mutate the graph while the native plan is retained. This restriction is the public ownership contract, not an incidental limitation. No separately supplied `Prepared` is admitted, no public CAS authentication operation is added, and no normal dependency edge changes. Tests use a development-only dependency on the `nrz-docgen` golden reader. The owner retains the original checked request and proof beside its native program. An original rational singleton can serve a represented native domain only if it equals that binary64 singleton exactly; other representations require a separately discharged input-import bound. Numeric literal containment does not establish nearest import.

**Rule (Native authority and resource separation)** · `rule:matrix:prepared-expm1-native`

`Expm1Evaluation` borrows the original prepared authority beside the numerical readings; retaining a result therefore also keeps its plan and frozen graph alive. Its constructor is private. Extracting a copy with `reading` yields numerical data, not a new authority. Neither result attachment nor the native loop constructs an exact certificate.

The native plan admits points and boxes in its represented subdomain at the prepared target. Ordered LU endpoints retain the relative endpoint guarantee; the independently evaluated nominal centre, total radius and numerical-only error have their separate meanings. Domain, below-normal operand, changed target, unsupported environment, malformed native program, bounded target-not-certifiable and exact resource failures remain distinct. CAS preparation and lowering happen once; `evaluate` reads neither graph nor exact proof. `evaluate_batch` admits counts and output/live byte capacities, prepays one Work unit per input inventory entry, then executes the native loop with no exact Work, CAS call or construction per cell. The caller's input array, returned exact evidence, frozen graph, native program and pending output vector coexist in the live-payload reservation. No partial result vector escapes an allocation or preflight failure. Per-cell numerical refusals remain explicit results in original order. This is a scalar-level bridge, not a region, mask, lowering-lambda or publication capability.

**Example (Native exponential evidence)** · `ex:matrix:prepared-expm1-evidence`

The public controls cover 71 distinct represented points in deliberately unsorted order and three additional boxes, including cancellation-sensitive powers, both domain endpoints, smallest-normal neighbours, zero-containing boxes and a subnormal nominal interior. Independent exact alternating sums bracket truth and independent binary encoding searches reconstruct round-to-nearest ties-to-even for nominal schedules. Endpoint containment and relative targets, nominal discrepancy and total coverage are checked separately (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-native-oracles`). The matrix test calls the docgen owner’s public bounded reader directly through a development-only dependency, then consumes `Payload::value` structurally. Each row supplies its own identity, input, golden interval and target; the distinct M1, M2 and M0 identities are asserted (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-validated-fixture`). Moving M1’s operation member to the end preserves those associations (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-fixture-member-order`). No nested Cargo process or duplicate payload checker participates. The deeper M2 scale is also independently reconstructed in the generated oracle test.

Domain, signed endpoint thresholds, exact zero and changed target controls isolate native causes (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-native-refusals`); original-domain and bounded-preparation controls retain the admission distinction (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-admission`). Exact-limit and one-below batch controls include seven prior Work, assert the full typed exhaustion, and retain earlier consumption (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-native-resources`). Separate rounded-program comparisons are regression checks, not independent truth (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-native-reuse`). The isolated `prepared_observation` integration target holds its shared dispatcher guard in every test, so no unguarded test in that process can reach the prepared callsites during interest registration. Its bounded subscriber observes native kernel spans and no construction or exact receipt traffic during reuse; enabled and disabled observation give the same results (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-observed-reuse`).

The constructor and batch prelude use `Cas::with_retained_graph` through bindings as a deliberate bookkeeping exception to the scalar completion boundary. Only CAS knows whether this ledger already registers this graph’s judgments. Its scoped callback counts those judgments once, preserves foreign owners and caller reservations, and restores capacity on either return while retaining Work. The returned authority copy and native program remain additional owners. No graph query reaches the cell loop. Eight distinct targets over one compact scope admit warm construction below the duplicate-judgment footprint, using an independently derived conservative allowance (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-constructor-ownership`). The warm-graph control reconstructs ownership from exact fixture inventories, independently of `owned_bytes`, and checks exact and one-below live boundaries on fresh and reused preparation ledgers beside a caller prefix (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-ledger-ownership`).

**Decision (Standalone producer scope)** · `dec:matrix:prepared-expm1-standalone-scope`

The public slice-and-`Vec` route performs no managed physical allocation. Before any allocation it covers, it checks complete logical accounting: `Cas::with_retained_graph` accounts for the graph, then the owned authority, native storage and input and output inventories remain covered. Input traversal is prepaid once; result order, per-cell refusals and the prepare-and-freeze lifetime are unchanged. The warm-graph exact and one-below controls remain this standalone route’s accounting evidence (`test:integration:prepared-expm1-ledger-ownership`). A future heap-aware or owned-input route requires its own single complete physical plan through common preflight, excluding inputs already funded and keeping the CAS retained registration outside that plan. This scoped exception grants no managed heap permit.

## Prepared exp composition · `sec:matrix:prepared-exp`

**Definition (Three exp outcomes and their evidence)** · `def:matrix:prepared-exp-outcomes`

`PreparedExp::new` accepts the existing CAS request encoding exp(argument)-1, with `bits` naming the desired exp absolute target. This permits the sole checked law to remain unchanged. The original exp operation is the addition of one to this authenticated basis, restored by exact algebra; no new CAS exp certificate is constructed. The constructor obtains the basis itself, retains its immutable graph borrow, and applies the same represented-domain correspondence as `PreparedExpm1`. The exp composition has three outcomes: `PreparedExpError::BasisPreparation` borrows the actual auxiliary request and records its magnitude-bound policy, and makes no claim about reachability of exp's absolute target; after a basis exists, `ExpFailure::TargetNotCertifiable` carries the failed endpoint index, checked point-image enclosure and outward width as binary64 encodings, and exp's requested absolute bits; success returns `ExpEvaluation`, with the original represented input, explicit exp readings, the absolute target and a borrow of the checked basis. The auxiliary policy remains separately inspectable and is not part of the final exp target claim. Domain, native validation and resource errors remain distinct from those numerical outcomes.

**Rule (Auxiliary target selection)** · `rule:matrix:prepared-exp-policy`

For exact rational upper bound B on |expm1|, choose the smallest integer k with 2^k >= B and request b+max(0,k) relative bits. On the admitted domain [-2,0], the cheap exact bound B=1/1 suffices, so k=0 and the actual auxiliary request has b bits. The implementation computes the integer comparison and checked target addition, rather than using an unexplained margin. Policy records contain B, k and the requested bits; exact powers below one exercise the negative-k clamp (`test:unit:prepared-exp-auxiliary-policy`). Positive inputs are outside the admitted domain, including the positive exp refusal row; this rule does not broaden the domain. The degree-one case at -2^-40 distinguishes basis failure from an exp impossibility using its independent alternating-series derivation (`test:integration:prepared-exp-basis-outcome`).

**Rule (Exp ownership and evidence)** · `rule:matrix:prepared-exp-evidence`

The scalar composition follows checked addition and absolute endpoint admission, including the nominal-centre reading; it retains the prepare-and-freeze contract and `Cas::with_retained_graph` bookkeeping. The standalone scope (`dec:matrix:prepared-expm1-standalone-scope`) also covers this slice-and-`Vec` producer. Its fixed composition storage, complete basis ownership and input/output inventories are admitted once, before allocation or traversal. Fresh and reused ledgers, a warm graph and a caller prefix have independently reconstructed exact and one-below boundaries (`test:integration:prepared-exp-resources`). The complete constructor output boundary includes both the existing CAS registration and the returned composition inventory (`test:integration:prepared-exp-constructor-output`); a direct guard control isolates the returned output boundary when the earlier CAS check dominates (`test:unit:prepared-exp-output-boundary`). Observation runs in the isolated `prepared_observation` target under the same shared guard and proves no construction or exact events in reuse, with no kernel span on prepaid Work refusal (`test:integration:prepared-exp-observed-reuse`). The composition uses the expm1 authority and CAS surface through the declared dependencies, without a managed physical plan.

Independent exact alternating sums and rational ties-to-even reconstruction test 73 distinct unsorted points and three boxes, including the exp image-midpoint and subnormal nominal interior (`test:integration:prepared-exp-independent-oracles`). Native controls distinguish exact zero, domain neighbours, signed below-normal magnitude, changed target and final spacing refusal using a checked basis (`test:integration:prepared-exp-native-refusals`). The public golden reader selects exp rows structurally and keeps E1, E2, E3, R2 and R4 with their own input and target (`test:integration:prepared-exp-golden-values`). A rational golden input is enclosed in binary64 neighbours; containment plus the entire composed image width proves the absolute slack at that original rational point. The R4 auxiliary request has no basis under the existing conservative law and reports basis failure, while a separate native test with an available basis isolates the final spacing refusal. Golden evidence is never re-derived in the fixture test.
